Customizing Elements
********************

.[perex]
Do you want an image with a `youtube:` address to render as a video, every heading to get a CSS class, code to be highlighted, or old links in your articles to fix themselves? You can hook into Texy's processing of the text at several points without touching its code. Everything here works the same for Markdown and for Texy syntax.


How Texy Processes Text
=======================

To know where to hook in, you only need to know the three stages the text goes through:

1. **Parsing.** Markdown or Texy syntax parses the text into a document tree. Every element of the text (a paragraph, a heading, a link or an image) is an object in it, called a node.
2. **Transformations of the document.** Texy fills in and adjusts the tree: it resolves where reference links point, repairs the HTML and applies typography. This is where you can add a transformation of your own, called a **pass**.
3. **Rendering.** The tree is turned into HTML. You can change how a particular node is rendered with your own **renderer**.

The difference between them matters. A pass changes **what is in the document**: a different link address, a removed image, counted words. A renderer changes only **how a node looks in HTML**: a different tag, an extra class, a video instead of an image. The whole process is described in detail in [Architecture and Principles |architecture].


Different HTML for an Element
=============================

A renderer is a function that Texy calls for every node of a certain kind and that returns its HTML. On our blog, we want an image with a `youtube:` address to render as a video player:

```php
use Texy\Nodes\ImageNode;
use Texy\Output\Html;

$texy = new Texy\Texy;
$markdown = new Texy\Markdown\Markdown(htmlOutput: $texy->htmlOutput);

$texy->htmlOutput->addRenderer(function (ImageNode $node, Html\Renderer $renderer, ?Closure $previous) {
	if (!str_starts_with($node->url ?? '', 'youtube:')) {
		return null; // Texy renders other images as usual
	}
	return new Html\Element('iframe', [
		'src' => 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/' . substr($node->url, 8),
		'width' => $node->width ?: 640,
		'height' => $node->height ?: 360,
		'allowfullscreen' => true,
	]);
});

$html = $markdown->toHtml('![Video about drying](youtube:dQw4w9WgXcQ)');
```

```html
<div><iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/dQw4w9WgXcQ" width="640"
height="360" allowfullscreen></iframe></div>
```

A renderer belongs to the HTML output settings, `htmlOutput`. Here Markdown shares them with `$texy`, as recommended in [Configuration |configuration], so the renderer applies to both languages: in Texy syntax it renders `[* youtube:dQw4w9WgXcQ *]` the same way.

Texy tells which nodes a renderer handles from the type of its first parameter, here `ImageNode`. The function receives three parameters:

- `$node` is the node to be rendered. Its properties, such as `$node->url` or `$node->width`, are listed in the [node overview |#Node Overview].
- `$renderer` is the object currently rendering the whole document. It comes in handy when you wrap the content of a node in your own tag: `$renderer->renderNodes($node->content->children)` renders it, and you put the result into the `children` property of your `Html\Element`.
- `$previous` is the renderer that would render the node if yours were not there. Usually it is Texy's default renderer.

You can return:

- `Html\Element`, an HTML tag with attributes in an array, which Texy outputs
- `Html\Raw`, a ready-made piece of HTML, which Texy outputs unchanged
- a string, plain text, which Texy escapes
- `null`, when you don't want to handle the node and `$previous` should render it. For a node of a [custom syntax |custom-syntax#Custom Node] that has no other renderer, `$previous` is `null` as well, and returning `null` throws an exception.


Adjusting the Default Result
----------------------------

Often you don't want to render the whole node yourself, only to adjust what Texy would produce. So you call `$previous($node)` and modify the result. This is how every heading of an article gets a CSS class:

```php
use Texy\Nodes\HeadingNode;

$texy->htmlOutput->addRenderer(function (HeadingNode $node, Html\Renderer $renderer, Closure $previous) {
	$el = $previous($node);
	$el->addClass('article-heading');
	return $el;
});
```

```html
<h1 class="article-heading">How We Dried Plums</h1>

<h2 class="article-heading">The Harvest</h2>
```

Renderers for the same kind of node form a chain. The one registered last is called first, and its `$previous` is the one registered before it, all the way down to Texy's default renderer. For built-in nodes, `$previous` is therefore never `null`, and you can write the parameter without the question mark, as here.


Highlighting Code
-----------------

Code blocks are `CodeBlockNode` nodes, with the code in `$node->code` and the language in `$node->language`. PHP code can be highlighted by the built-in function `highlight_string()`, which returns finished HTML, so we return it as `Html\Raw`:

```php
use Texy\Nodes\CodeBlockNode;

$texy->htmlOutput->addRenderer(function (CodeBlockNode $node, Html\Renderer $renderer, ?Closure $previous) {
	if ($node->language !== 'php') {
		return null;
	}
	return new Html\Raw(highlight_string($node->code, return: true));
});
```

This highlights a <code>&#96;&#96;&#96;php</code> block in Markdown as well as in Texy syntax, where it can also be written as `/--code php`. Blocks in other languages are rendered by Texy as usual, as `<pre class="language-xxx"><code>`, and the language class is handy for highlighting in the browser, too. For other languages, you can plug in any code highlighting library the same way.

.[note]
A renderer must not change the node it receives: the same document can be rendered several times and must give the same HTML every time. When you need a modified copy, clone the node; `clone $node` copies everything inside it as well.


Modifying the Document
======================

A pass is a function that receives the whole document after parsing and may change it in any way. The change then applies to everything produced from the document.

The blog has moved, and old articles link to its pages using the absolute address `http://my-blog.com/…`. A pass turns them into site-relative addresses:

```php
use Texy\Nodes;

$fixLinks = function (Nodes\DocumentNode $document): void {
	foreach ($document->find(Nodes\LinkNode::class) as $link) {
		if ($link->url !== null && str_starts_with($link->url, 'http://my-blog.com/')) {
			$link->url = substr($link->url, strlen('http://my-blog.com'));
		}
	}
};

$markdown->addPass($fixLinks);  // for articles in Markdown
$texy->addPass($fixLinks);      // for articles in Texy syntax
```

```html
<p>See the <a href="/recipe">recipe</a> and <a
href="https://example.com">elsewhere</a>.</p>
```

`find()` returns all nodes of the given kind in the order they appear in the text. As the second parameter, you can pass a function that decides for each node found whether you want it, for example `$document->find(Nodes\LinkNode::class, fn($link) => $link->url === null)`. `findFirst()` returns only the first one found, or `null`. Every node has both methods, so you can also search inside a single paragraph.

Unlike renderers, a pass is registered with the language: on `$markdown` for Markdown, on `$texy` for Texy syntax. A pass does not edit the source text; the file with the article stays unchanged. To rewrite the source text as well, see the [Source Text Tools |source-tools] page.


Replacing and Removing Nodes
----------------------------

When you need to replace a node with another one or remove it, walk the tree with the `traverse()` method. We don't want images in blog comments, but we do want readers to know what the author put there. The pass replaces every image with text containing its description:

```php
$comments = new Texy\Markdown\Markdown;
$comments->addPass(function (Nodes\DocumentNode $document): void {
	$document->traverse(fn(Texy\Node $node) => $node instanceof Nodes\ImageNode
		? new Nodes\TextNode('[image: ' . $node->alt . ']')
		: null);
});
```

```html
<p>Take a look: [image: my dryer] great.</p>
```

The function passed to `traverse()` receives each node in turn. When it returns a different node, that node replaces the original; when it returns `null`, the node is left alone. It can also return one of the values of the `Texy\TraverseAction` enum: `Remove` removes the node, `SkipChildren` skips its contents and `Stop` ends the walk. Only a node in a list of siblings can be removed, such as a paragraph in the document or a node in the text of a paragraph. A node in a fixed place, such as the `$content` of a paragraph, cannot be removed, and `Remove` throws an exception.

As the second parameter of `traverse()`, you can pass a function that Texy calls for each node only after it has walked through its contents. It can return the same values.


When a Pass Runs
----------------

Passes run after Texy's built-in transformations and before typography, so any text you insert still gets typeset. When you need a different moment, pass a second parameter:

```php
$texy->addPass($pass, Texy\Stage::Finalize);
```

- `Stage::Transform`, the default, after the built-in transformations and before typography
- `Stage::Finalize` after typography, when you want to see the final text
- `Stage::Syntax` right during parsing, for tools that work with the source text, see [Source Text Tools |source-tools]

At the default stage, headings already have their levels and ids computed, and the HTML has been checked against the allowed tags. A heading inserted by a pass therefore gets no id. When you need to add headings to the document, convert the text step by step as shown in [Configuration |configuration#Headings], and insert the headings after `parse()` and before `transform()`.


Directives
==========

The construct `{{name: value}}` in Texy syntax is a directive. On its own it does nothing; you give it its meaning. On our blog, we want `{{author: jane}}` to create a link to the author's profile:

```php
$texy->config->directiveHandler = fn(Nodes\DirectiveNode $directive) => match (strtolower($directive->name)) {
	'author' => new Nodes\LinkNode(
		url: '/user/' . $directive->value,
		content: Nodes\ContentNode::fromText('@' . $directive->value),
	),
	default => $directive,
};
```

```texy
This article was written by {{author: jane}}.
```

```html
<p>This article was written by <a href="/user/jane">@jane</a>.</p>
```

There is one function for all directives, and Texy calls it for each of them except `{{texy: …}}`. It receives the directive node: the name is in `$directive->name`, the value after the colon in `$directive->value` and the comma-separated arguments in the `$directive->args` array. The node it returns is inserted into the document in place of the directive. When it returns the directive itself, as in the `default` branch here, the directive stays in the text exactly as the author wrote it. When it returns anything else, such as `null`, the directive is removed.

Without the function, all directives stay in the text. To find out about those that nobody handles, for example because of the author's typo, note them down in the function:

```php
$unknown = [];
$texy->config->directiveHandler = function (Nodes\DirectiveNode $directive) use (&$unknown) {
	if (strtolower($directive->name) === 'author') {
		return new Nodes\LinkNode(
			url: '/user/' . $directive->value,
			content: Nodes\ContentNode::fromText('@' . $directive->value),
		);
	}
	$unknown[] = $directive->name;  // note down the names of unknown directives
	return $directive;              // the directive stays in the text
};
```

Directives run as the very first transformation of the document. That is why they can also change the settings used to process the rest of the document, for example the heading level in `$texy->config->headingTop`. Such a change, however, belongs to the `$texy` object, not to the document, and it stays in effect for later conversions too. If your directives make such changes, convert each document with its own `Texy\Texy` object.


Which Approach to Choose
========================

- **Only the look of the HTML changes** (a different tag, a class, a video instead of an image): a renderer.
- **The content of the document changes** (an address, a removed element, collected data): a pass. It then applies to every output, plain text included.
- **The author writes something and you handle it** in Texy syntax: a directive.
- **The author writes a completely new syntax**, such as `@@jane`: [custom syntax |custom-syntax].


Node Overview
=============

Nodes live in the `Texy\Nodes` namespace. Markdown and Texy syntax create the same ones, so a renderer or a pass works for both languages.

|---
| Node | Element | Important properties
|---
| `DocumentNode` | the whole document | `$content`, `$meta`
| `ParagraphNode` | paragraph | `$content`, `$modifier`
| `HeadingNode` | heading | `$level`, `$id`, `$content`, `getTitle()`
| `ListNode`, `ListItemNode` | list and its item | `$items`, `$type`, `$start`; item `$content`, `$checked` in a task list
| `DefinitionListNode` | definition list | `$items`
| `TableNode`, `TableRowNode`, `TableCellNode` | table, row, cell | table `$rows`, `$caption`; cell `$content`, `$colspan`, `$rowspan`, `$header`
| `BlockQuoteNode` | blockquote | `$content`
| `CodeBlockNode` | code block | `$code`, `$language`
| `PreformattedNode` | preformatted text, a `/--` block without a type | `$content`
| `HtmlBlockNode` | HTML block, in Markdown and in Texy syntax (`/--html`) | `$content`
| `TextBlockNode`, `CommentNode`, `TexySourceNode` | `/--text`, `/--comment` and `/--texysource` blocks |
| `HorizontalRuleNode` | horizontal rule | `$type`
| `DivNode` | `/--div` block | `$content`, `$modifier`
| `UnknownDelimitedNode` | a `/--` block with a type nobody handles, kept whole, exactly as written | `$code`, `$type`
| `FigureNode` | an image with a caption, or an image alone on a line | `$image` (`ImageNode`, or `LinkNode` when the image is a link), `$caption`, `$float`
| `TextNode` | text | `$text`
| `PhraseNode` | emphasized text | `$type` (the `PhraseType` enum, e.g. `PhraseType::Strong`, `PhraseType::Emphasis`), `$content`
| `AnnotationNode` | text with an explanation, rendered as `<abbr title>`; no built-in syntax creates it | `$text`, `$annotation`
| `InlineCodeNode` | inline code | `$code`
| `LinkNode` | link | `$url`, `$content`
| `UrlNode`, `EmailNode` | a URL and an email address written directly in Texy syntax |
| `ImageNode` | image | `$url`, `$alt` (description), `$width`, `$height`, `$float`
| `LineBreakNode`, `SoftBreakNode` | a line break, and in Markdown an ordinary line end within a paragraph |
| `HtmlTagNode`, `HtmlElementNode`, `HtmlCommentNode` | an HTML tag and comment written in the text | `$name`, `$attributes`
| `RawTextNode` | processing instructions and declarations in HTML within Markdown, shown as text | `$text`
| `DirectiveNode` | `{{…}}` directive | `$name`, `$value`, `$args`
| `EmoticonNode` | emoticon |

A node with `$modifier` carries the classes, ID, styles and title from a [modifier |syntax#Modifiers] in a `Texy\Modifier` object: `$classes`, `$id`, `$styles`, `$attrs`, `$title`.

A node that has no renderer is not reported as an error in HTML or plain text; Texy renders only its content. Thanks to this, a document can always be rendered into these outputs, even when it contains a node that only one extension knows about. Printing the document back to source text rejects such a node, as described in [Source Text Tools |source-tools#Replacing a Whole Element].

Coming from Texy 3, where behavior was changed with handlers and events? How to rewrite them as renderers and passes is described in [Upgrading from Texy 3 |upgrading].

{{maintitle: Customizing Elements in Texy: Renderers, Passes and Directives}}
{{description: Change how Texy renders images, links, headings or code blocks, modify the document before rendering and handle directives. For Markdown and Texy syntax.}}
